---
import "../styles/global.css";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Progwise is building the next-generation platform for developers and their customers to create, maintain, and evolve software with AI agents, engineering guardrails, and human expertise."
    />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#ffffff" />
    <meta
      property="og:title"
      content="Progwise — You shape. Agents build. Experts guide."
    />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="Explore the platform Progwise is building for developers and their customers to create, maintain, and evolve software with AI."
    />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://www.progwise.net/" />
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.progwise.net/" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <title>Progwise — You shape. Agents build. Experts guide.</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#main">
      Skip to content
    </a>
    <header class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="#" aria-label="Progwise home">
        <img
          class="brand-signet"
          src="/planet-progwise.svg"
          width="28"
          height="29"
          alt=""
        />
        progwise
      </a>
      <nav aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="#platform">The platform</a>
        <a href="#how">How it works</a>
        <a href="#contact">Let’s talk</a>
      </nav>
    </header>
    <main id="main">
      <section class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">The next chapter of building software</p>
        <h1>You shape. Agents build. Experts guide.</h1>
        <p class="intro">
          A shared platform for developers and their customers to create,
          maintain, and evolve software with AI.
        </p>
        <p>
          Software development is becoming a business-driven process. The people
          who know the business decide what the software should do. Developers
          and designers are the experts they bring in.
        </p>
        <p class="status">
          The platform is in development at Progwise. This is the experience
          we’re building.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our ambition is a first version in days, followed by years of
          continuous improvement, with lower delivery and maintenance costs.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="platform">
        <h2>Your projects. Your customers. One shared process.</h2>
        <h3>For developers and software companies</h3>
        <p>
          Run your customer projects in one place. Code, pipelines, and
          deployments are hosted on the platform, built on Git. Set the
          engineering standards once, invite customers and designers, and bring
          your expertise where it’s needed.
        </p>
        <p>
          We’re starting with a few supported tech stacks, so agents, pipelines,
          and deployments work the same way in every project. Bring existing
          projects with their full Git history.
        </p>
        <h3>For business people and designers</h3>
        <p>
          Describe what you need in your own words. Your AI consultant asks
          questions, suggests ideas, and prepares decisions you can approve,
          decline, or refine. Your development team handles technical choices
          and explains their impact on cost and timing.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="how">
        <h2>From the first request to the next release.</h2>
        <p>
          The same steps take a project from its first version through every
          improvement. Everyday changes stay in the business team’s hands,
          without hiring a developer for every amendment.
        </p>
        <figure class="lifecycle">
          <figcaption>How a project evolves</figcaption>
          <ol role="list">
            <li>
              <strong>Start the project.</strong> The developer sets the
              engineering rules and invites the customer.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Request.</strong> Describe a need, a problem, or an idea.
              The AI consultant asks questions and proposes an outcome.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Agreed work.</strong> Agree what will be done and what
              counts as done.
              <span class="lifecycle-note">
                Waiting for a decision or funding? Work resumes when it’s
                available.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Change.</strong> Agents build it, run the checks, and
              prepare a preview. The customer approves the outcome; developers
              review the code where required.
              <span class="lifecycle-note">
                Needs revision? The same change is updated and reviewed again.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Release.</strong> The approved change goes live and is
              recorded.
              <span class="lifecycle-note">
                The next improvement starts again at Request.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="lifecycle-archive">
            <strong>Archive the project.</strong> Active work ends. Decisions,
            changes, and release history are kept.
          </p>
        </figure>
        <p class="lifecycle-terms">
          For developers: agreed work is an issue, a change is a branch and pull
          request, and approval is a merge.
        </p>
        <details>
          <summary>How we plan to protect existing software and data</summary>
          <p>
            Limited agent permissions, isolated previews, automated tests, and
            specialist review provide safeguards. Backups, tested data
            migrations, and verified recovery procedures protect existing data.
            Release plans cover monitoring and recovery for both software and
            databases.
          </p>
        </details>
        <h3>Meet in the project office</h3>
        <p>
          Meetings take place in a shared virtual office with rooms and people
          you can walk up to. Review previews together and turn conversations
          into recorded decisions and work.
        </p>
        <h3>Keep control of spending</h3>
        <p>
          Project work is funded monthly in advance and starts after payment.
          The platform tracks spending, updates forecasts, and warns when funds
          run low. Chargeable work pauses when funding runs out, and additional
          spending needs your agreement.
        </p>
        <p class="status">
          Project funding is separate from the platform subscription. Platform
          pricing is still being developed.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="section" id="contact">
        <h2>Help shape what comes next.</h2>
        <p>
          Build software for customers, or shape the tools your business uses?
          We’d like to hear how you work.
        </p>
        <a href="mailto:info@progwise.net?subject=The%20next%20chapter%20of%20building%20software">
          info@progwise.net
        </a>
      </section>
    </main>
    <footer class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="#">
        <img
          class="brand-signet"
          src="/planet-progwise.svg"
          width="28"
          height="29"
          alt=""
        />
        progwise
      </a>
      <span>© {new Date().getFullYear()} Progwise Ltd.</span>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
